    "q-214969": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214969",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{4}{3z-4}=\\frac{2}{z}.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $z=0$ and $z=\\frac{4}{3}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3z-4} & =\\frac{2}{z} \\\\ 4\\cdot z & =(3z-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 4z & =6z-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z & =6z-8 \\\\ 4z-6z & =6z-8-6z \\\\ -2z & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{-2z}{-2} & =\\frac{-8}{-2} \\\\ z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z=4$ is different from $z=0$ and $z=\\frac{4}{3}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $z=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
