    "q-215066": {
      "dom_id": "step-q215066",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{4}{3x-5}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=0$  cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3x-5} & =-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3x-5} & =\\frac{-2}{x} \\\\ 4\\cdot x & =(3x-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 4x & =-6x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =-6x+10 \\\\ 4x+6x & =-6x+10+6x \\\\ 10x & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & =\\frac{10}{10} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=1$ is different from $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
          "dom_index": 0,
          "disabled": false,
