    "q-215111": {
      "dom_id": "step-q215111",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Solve for $y$ where $\\frac{3}{y}=-\\frac{2}{y+5}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $y=0$ and $y=-5$  cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y} & =-\\frac{2}{y+5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{y} & =\\frac{-2}{y+5} \\\\ 3\\cdot (y+5) & =y\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 3y+15 & =-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+15 & =-2y \\\\ 3y+15-3y & =-2y-3y \\\\ 15 & =-5y \\\\ \\frac{15}{-5} & =\\frac{-5y}{-5} \\\\ -3 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $y=-3$ is different from $y=0$ and $y=-5$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $y=-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
