    "q-215142": {
      "dom_id": "step-q215142",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{2}{x+8}=\\frac{3}{5-2x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=-8$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x+8} & =\\frac{3}{5-2x} \\\\ 2\\cdot (5-2x) & =(x+8)\\cdot 3 \\\\ 10-4x & =3x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-4x & =3x+24 \\\\ 10-4x+4x & =3x+24+4x \\\\ 10 & =7x+24 \\\\ 10-24 & =7x+24-24 \\\\ -14 & =7x \\\\ \\frac{-14}{7} & =\\frac{7x}{7} \\\\ -2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-2$ is different from $x=-8$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $x=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
