    "q-102820": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102820",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{y+2}=\\frac{3}{2y+5}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $y=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $y=-2$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y+2} & =\\frac{3}{2y+5} \\\\ 1\\cdot (2y+5) & =(y+2)\\cdot 3 \\\\ 2y+5 & =3y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+5 & =3y+6 \\\\ 2y+5-2y & =3y+6-2y \\\\ 5 & =y+6 \\\\ 5-6 & =y+6-6 \\\\ -1 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y=-1$ is different from $y=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $y=-2,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $y=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
