    "q-212151": {
      "dom_id": "step-q212151",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{1}{x-2}=\\frac{2}{x+4}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=-4$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2} & =\\frac{2}{x+4} \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+4) & =(x-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+4 & =2x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =2x-4 \\\\ x+4-x & =2x-4-x \\\\ 4 & =x-4 \\\\ 4+4 & =x-4+4 \\\\ 8 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=8$ is different from $x=-4$ and $x=2,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $x=8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
